before:
setActive(button,active) receives an existing button and Boolean, updates its attribute/class, and returns the token’s final state. querySelector("#toggle") returns the first matching element or null; this supplied markup guarantees a match.
```javascript
function setActive(button, active) {
  button.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(active));
  return button.classList.toggle("active", active);
}
const button = document.querySelector("#toggle");
console.log(setActive(button, true));
console.log(button.getAttribute("aria-pressed"));
console.log(button.classList.contains("control"), button.classList.contains("active"));
console.log(setActive(button, false));
```
Output: true; true; true true; false. The second line is attribute text, even though it looks like the first line’s Boolean.

after:
Here is the complete example. Run it on a page with the button above.
```javascript
function setActive(button, active) {
  button.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(active));
  return button.classList.toggle("active", active);
}
const button = document.querySelector("#toggle");
console.log(setActive(button, true));
console.log(button.getAttribute("aria-pressed"));
console.log(button.classList.contains("control"), button.classList.contains("active"));
console.log(setActive(button, false));
```
Output:
```text
true
true
true true
false
```
`setActive` updates the button and returns its class state. `querySelector("#toggle")` finds the button. The second output line comes from an attribute, so it is text.
